>
  <h2>What it is</h2>

  <p>
    CoreFlow is a business ERP for small and mid-sized companies that have outgrown spreadsheets and
    a drawer full of disconnected tools. It covers <strong>CRM, inventory, sales, procurement,
    finance and approvals</strong> as one system rather than six integrations.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the sales description. The engineering description is
    <strong>24 feature modules</strong> — master data, pricing, procurement, returns, advanced
    inventory, finance and financial accounting, documents, exports, reporting, search,
    notifications, collaboration, governance, security, system health and more — over
    <strong>24 domain aggregates</strong>, across seven backend projects and six test suites.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is my own product — architecture, build and positioning. That matters for this case study,
    because it means every trade-off below was mine to get right or wrong, with no architect above me
    to overrule a shortcut.
  </p>

  <h2>The actual hard problem</h2>

  <p>
    Writing an inventory module is not difficult. Writing an inventory module that sales,
    procurement and finance all need to read from — without any of them reaching directly into its
    tables — is where ERPs are won or lost.
  </p>

  <p>
    The failure mode is well known and slow: module A queries module B's tables because it is faster
    today, and eighteen months later no change is safe because nothing has a boundary. For a small
    team this is fatal, since there is no headcount to absorb that kind of coupling.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Every decision below is aimed at one thing: making the wrong kind of coupling harder to write
      than the right kind.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The decisions</h2>

  <h3>Clean Architecture layering</h3>
  <p>
    Domain, Application, Infrastructure and API as distinct layers, with dependencies pointing
    inward only. The domain knows nothing about EF Core, HTTP or PostgreSQL. This is not
    architectural decoration — it is what lets a module be reasoned about, and tested, without
    standing up the rest of the system.
  </p>

  <h3>CQRS separation</h3>
  <p>
    Reads and writes have genuinely different shapes in an ERP. A write needs domain rules, invariants
    and validation; a report needs to join across six tables and return fast. Forcing both through
    one model makes each worse.
  </p>
  <p>
    So writes go through the domain model, and reads use <strong>Dapper projections</strong> that
    return exactly the shape the screen needs. The result is that a slow report never forces a
    compromise in the domain model, and a domain rule never has to be bent for a query.
  </p>

  <h3>Transactional outbox for cross-module events</h3>
  <p>
    When sales confirms an order, inventory must know. The naive version does both writes in one
    place and hopes. The outbox pattern commits the event in the same transaction as the business
    change, then dispatches it separately — so modules communicate through events rather than
    through each other's tables, and a crash between the two steps cannot lose the message.
  </p>

  <h3>Redis hybrid caching</h3>
  <p>
    ERP screens read the same reference data constantly — product catalogues, tax rates, approval
    chains. HybridCache keeps hot data in memory with a Valkey/Redis L2 behind it, so the read path
    stays fast across instances without every node holding its own divergent copy. The same Redis
    also backs Data Protection and the SignalR backplane, which is what makes running more than one
    replica safe rather than merely possible.
  </p>

  <h3>A generated API client, not a hand-written one</h3>
  <p>
    The Angular 22 frontend consumes an <strong>OpenAPI/NSwag-generated client</strong> rather than
    hand-written services. Hand-written API clients drift from the backend the moment someone is in a
    hurry, and the drift surfaces at runtime in front of a customer. Generating the client means a
    contract change breaks the build instead — which is exactly where it should break.
  </p>
  <p>
    The contract is also checked against an <strong>immutable baseline</strong>, so changes have to be
    additive. An ERP customer does not upgrade their integrations because I shipped on a Tuesday.
  </p>

  <h3>An API surface designed for clients that cannot redeploy in lockstep</h3>
  <p>
    Three decisions do most of the work here. Updates round-trip an <strong>opaque concurrency
    token</strong> and a stale one returns 409 rather than silently overwriting someone else's edit —
    in finance that distinction is the whole game. High-impact operations require an
    <strong>Idempotency-Key</strong>, so a retried request after a timeout does not post a second
    invoice. And errors are <strong>RFC ProblemDetails</strong> carrying an error code, trace ID and
    correlation ID, so a support conversation starts from an identifier instead of a screenshot.
  </p>

  <h3>Migrations that never run themselves</h3>
  <p>
    A separate <strong>DatabaseMigrator</strong> project owns schema changes, and the API never
    migrates production automatically. You run a dry-run, read it, then apply with an explicit flag.
    Automatic migration on startup is convenient right up to the morning it is not.
  </p>

  <h3>ASP.NET Core Identity</h3>
  <p>
    Authentication and authorisation on a standard, well-audited foundation. An ERP holds a company's
    financial data; rolling my own auth would be the least defensible decision in the entire system.
  </p>

  <h2>What this bought</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A module can be changed, or read in isolation, without tracing its dependencies through five
      others.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reporting performance and domain correctness stopped competing with each other.
    </li>
    <li>
      Backend and frontend cannot silently disagree about the API contract.
    </li>
    <li>
      The layering is enforceable — architecture rules fail the build rather than relying on everyone
      remembering, and Husky runs the gate before a commit lands.
    </li>
    <li>
      Six test projects cover unit, domain, architecture, contract, API integration and PostgreSQL
      integration, so "it compiles" is not the standard for a change.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it stands</h2>

  <p>
    CoreFlow is in progress and pre-launch. The product site is public and the platform itself is
    private, since it is intended to be sold rather than given away. I would rather say that plainly
    than present an unfinished product as a shipped one.
  </p>
</CaseLayout>
